From dc4742fd29b162fc5ff4de2e7a77d5e12d0ea3ae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 12:40:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/8] feat(registry): record allocations per resource uid with
 exclusive devices

Allocations did not record the devices they hold, and the only
exclusivity guarantee was a unique index on resource_id that ignored
tenants. Nothing in the database stopped two allocations from holding
the same device, and the same resource id could not hold an allocation
in two tenants.

- allocations.resource_id holds the owning resource's uid. At most one
  Active allocation per (tenant_id, resource_id) is enforced by the new
  partial index idx_allocations_one_active_per_tenant_resource; opening
  an existing database drops the old global index and keeps its rows.
- New allocation_devices table. A partial unique index keeps every
  (node_id, device_id) in at most one Active allocation, and a trigger
  keeps device rows in step with their allocation's status, so
  releasing or expiring an allocation frees its devices in the same
  statement.
- create_allocation checks the owner (exists, same uid, not
  Terminating), the idempotency key (a replay returns the existing
  allocation, a used key is never reused), the owner's active
  allocation and the devices, and writes the allocation with its
  devices, all in one BEGIN IMMEDIATE transaction.
- get_allocation, get_allocation_by_idempotency_key, allocated_devices
  and release_active_allocation serve the scheduler and the delete
  path; allocation records now list their devices.
